   Scratch that. You said "player-monsters," not "monsters." I suppose
that means no ring-bearing vortices, but it also means no ring-bearing
soldiers or gnomish wizards. That's a direction for enhancement.


   While updating my page with a list of your changes, I noticed that
it looks like you're bringing the behavior of =oTele in line with its
effect on the player --- 1/85 chance of teleporting, unless the wearer
is a tengu. I think that's overzealous pursuit of realism; would that
even show any in-game effect? Most monsters /I/ meet that could benefit
from =oTele are dead within 10 turns. I'd keep the discrepancy here.
   Tongue-in-cheek rationale: The ring of teleport interacts with the
wearer's metabolic rate, so creatures with faster metabolisms teleport
more often. And we all know that animals with short lives (mice, rabbits) 
have faster metabolisms than long-lived animals (humans, elephants).
Since the average dungeon denizen has a /very/ short life, it should
be teleporting all the time!

   Also, I'd postulate that while a monster might not be able to tell
whether the player can see invisible or not, at least it'd be able to
tell as soon as it put on a ring of invisibility. So it would be a
very silly monster that put on a useless ring of invisibility and then 
kept it on despite having a useful ring in its inventory. If the only
way to avoid that very silly situation is to cheat and prevent monsters
from putting on useless =oInvis in the first place, I say "cheat!"

